  "description": "Return a new flat list containing the integers from a nested list of integers. Nesting can be any depth; preserve left-to-right order. The input contains only lists and integers and must remain unchanged.",
  "starterCode": "def flatten_nested(items):\n    # Return all integers in nested-list order\n    pass\n",
  "referenceSolution": "def flatten_nested(items):\n    flattened = []\n    for item in items:\n        if isinstance(item, list):\n            flattened.extend(flatten_nested(item))\n        else:\n            flattened.append(item)\n    return flattened",
  "solution": {
    "code": "def flatten_nested(items):\n    flattened = []\n    for item in items:\n        if isinstance(item, list):\n            flattened.extend(flatten_nested(item))\n        else:\n            flattened.append(item)\n    return flattened",
    "explanation": "Use recursion for nested lists: add a scalar directly, and recursively flatten each child list. The output list keeps the original left-to-right order. Empty nested lists naturally add no values."
  },
  "wrongSolutions": [
    "def flatten_nested(items):\n    return [item for group in items for item in group]",
    "def flatten_nested(items):\n    return sorted({value for item in items for value in item})"
